# Test: the looking glass peers page shows session history, and its live stream follows it
# QUESTION: after a real session dropped, does an operator read on /lg/peers
#           how often it dropped, when it last changed and why it closed, and
#           does the page they keep open learn of the next drop?
# VALIDATES: one peer, the scripted test peer, closes its session with a
#            NOTIFICATION as soon as the first End-of-RIB arrives. Once the
#            session is back, GET /lg/peers (handleUIPeers and extractPeers,
#            internal/component/lg/handler_ui.go) shows in the peer's row the
#            `state-changed` time and a `connections-dropped` count of 1, both
#            equal to what `show bgp` answers, and the row or the page it opens,
#            /lg/peer/127.0.0.1, shows the `last-error` text `show bgp` answers
#            (spec-lg-peer-history-origin-search-and-named-nodes AC-1).
#            The fixture then opens /lg/events as an open page does, announces
#            one route, which the peer answers with a second NOTIFICATION, and
#            reads stream messages until one carries the peer's row with a
#            count of 2 (handleUIEvents and peerStreamBody, AC-2).
# PREVENTS: a session that drops every hour looking like one that has been up
#           for an hour, because the page reads uptime and none of the history
#           keys the engine publishes.
#
# WHY A FUNCTIONAL TEST: the package tests feed extractPeers a summary written
# by hand. Only a real session proves that the keys the reactor fills when a
# peer sends a NOTIFICATION are the ones the page reads, and that the stream
# sends the row again after the count moved.
#
# THE PAGE CONTRACT THIS TEST READS: the two history cells of a peer row are
# <td class="state-changed"> and <td class="connections-dropped">. The class
# is what tells the drop count from the other numbers of the row.
#
# NO SLEEP: the first drop is the peer's, on the first End-of-RIB. The fixture
# (internal/test/fixture/lg_peer_history.go) waits on `show bgp` for one drop
# and a second End-of-RIB on the wire, which is the session back up, and that
# state holds until the fixture itself announces the route that triggers the
# second drop. The page is therefore read over a state that does not move.

# ze-peer: two sessions. The first ends on the End-of-RIB. The second ends on
# the UPDATE the fixture sends once the stream is open.
stdin=peer:terminator=EOF_PEER
option=tcp_connections:value=2
option=timeout:value=70s

expect=bgp:conn=1:seq=1:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF00170200000000
action=notification:conn=1:seq=2:text=Peer Reset

expect=bgp:conn=2:seq=1:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF00170200000000
expect=bgp:conn=2:seq=2:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF002F0200000015400101004002004003040101010140050400000064100101
action=notification:conn=2:seq=3:text=Peer Reset
EOF_PEER

# Pass the looking-glass listen port ($PORT2) to the plugin process.
option=env:var=ZE_LG_PORT:value=$PORT2

stdin=ze-bgp:terminator=EOF_CONF
plugin {
	external lg-peer-history {
		run "le test fixture plugin/lg-peer-history"
		encoder json
	}
	internal bgp-rib {
		use bgp-rib
	}
}

environment {
	looking-glass {
		enabled true
		server main {
			ip 127.0.0.1
			port $PORT2
			# This test asserts looking-glass CONTENT over plain HTTP, so it takes
			# the documented TLS opt-out.
			tls false
		}
	}
}

bgp {
	peer peer1 {
		connection {
			remote {
				ip 127.0.0.1
			}
			local {
				ip 127.0.0.1
				accept false
			}
		}
		session {
			asn {
				local 1
				remote 1
			}
			router-id 1.2.3.4
			family {
				ipv4/unicast { prefix { maximum 10000; } }
			}
			capability {
				graceful-restart disable
			}
		}
		behavior {
			group-updates disable
		}
		timer { connect-retry 5; }

		attach process lg-peer-history {
			send [ update ]
		}
		attach process bgp-rib {
			receive [ update state refresh ]
			send [ update ]
		}
	}
}
EOF_CONF

cmd=background:seq=1:exec=le test peer --port $PORT:stdin=peer
cmd=foreground:seq=2:exec=ze -:stdin=ze-bgp:timeout=90s
expect=exit:code=0
# The fixture prints this only after the page showed the first drop and the
# stream carried the second; a failed check returns an error, which the runner
# reads as the test's failure.
expect=stderr:pattern=OK: the peers page showed one drop, its time .* and the last error .*, and the live stream carried the second drop
